### Why can you not use a single ACID transaction across services?

Each service owns its own database. A two-phase commit across independent services is blocking, holds locks, and couples availability. The accepted pattern is the Saga: break the operation into local transactions, each committing in one service, and define a compensating action for each step.

```java
package com.bank.order.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

public class Order {
    private String orderId;
    private String customerId;  // e.g. "CUST-RAJESH-001"
    private BigDecimal totalAmount;
    private String status;      // CREATED, INVENTORY_RESERVED, PAYMENT_CHARGED, CONFIRMED, FAILED
    private String idempotencyKey;
    private Instant createdAt;

    public Order(String orderId, String customerId, BigDecimal totalAmount,
                 String idempotencyKey) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.totalAmount = totalAmount;
        this.idempotencyKey = idempotencyKey;
        this.status = "CREATED";
        this.createdAt = Instant.now();
    }

    // Getters omitted for brevity
    public String getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public String getStatus() { return status; }
    public void setStatus(String status) { this.status = status; }
}
```

### Where do transaction boundaries live?

Transaction boundaries live inside each service, never across them. The order service wraps its create in a local transaction. Payment wraps the debit. Inventory wraps the reservation. What spans services is the Saga logic and messaging.

```java
package com.bank.order.service;

import com.bank.order.model.Order;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
    private final OutboxRepository outboxRepository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository orderRepository,
                        OutboxRepository outboxRepository) {
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
        this.outboxRepository = outboxRepository;
    }

    // Single local transaction: create order + write outbox event
    public Order createOrder(String customerId, BigDecimal amount,
                             String idempotencyKey) {
        // Idempotency check
        Order existing = orderRepository.findByIdempotencyKey(idempotencyKey);
        if (existing != null) {
            return existing; // Return stored result, no duplicate
        }

        Order order = new Order(generateId(), customerId, amount, idempotencyKey);
        orderRepository.save(order);              // local DB write
        outboxRepository.save("OrderCreated", order.getOrderId()); // same txn
        return order;
    }

    private String generateId() {
        return "ORD-" + System.currentTimeMillis();
    }
}
```

### How does the Saga handle payment and inventory failures?

The Saga steps are: reserve inventory, charge payment, confirm order. If any step fails, compensations run in reverse.

| Step | Service | Compensation |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Reserve inventory | Inventory | Release reservation |
| 2. Charge payment | Payment | Refund payment |
| 3. Confirm order | Order | Mark order FAILED |

```java
package com.bank.order.saga;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderSagaOrchestrator {

    private final InventoryClient inventoryClient;
    private final PaymentClient paymentClient;
    private final OrderService orderService;

    public OrderSagaOrchestrator(InventoryClient inventoryClient,
                                  PaymentClient paymentClient,
                                  OrderService orderService) {
        this.inventoryClient = inventoryClient;
        this.paymentClient = paymentClient;
        this.orderService = orderService;
    }

    public void execute(String orderId, String customerId,
                        String productId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Step 1: Reserve inventory
        boolean reserved = inventoryClient.reserve(orderId, productId);
        if (!reserved) {
            orderService.markFailed(orderId, "INVENTORY_UNAVAILABLE");
            return;
        }

        // Step 2: Charge payment
        boolean charged = paymentClient.charge(orderId, customerId, amount);
        if (!charged) {
            // Compensation: release inventory
            inventoryClient.releaseReservation(orderId, productId);
            orderService.markFailed(orderId, "PAYMENT_FAILED");
            return;
        }

        // Step 3: Confirm
        orderService.confirm(orderId);
    }
}
```

### How does synchronous vs asynchronous flow work?

Create the order synchronously so the customer gets an order ID immediately. Drive payment and inventory through Kafka as a Saga. Notify asynchronously.

```java
package com.bank.order.controller;

import com.bank.order.model.Order;
import com.bank.order.service.OrderService;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderController {

    private final OrderService orderService;
    private final KafkaPublisher kafkaPublisher;

    public OrderController(OrderService orderService, KafkaPublisher kafkaPublisher) {
        this.orderService = orderService;
        this.kafkaPublisher = kafkaPublisher;
    }

    // Synchronous: customer gets order ID immediately
    public Order placeOrder(String customerId, BigDecimal amount,
                            String idempotencyKey) {
        Order order = orderService.createOrder(customerId, amount, idempotencyKey);
        // Async: Saga starts via Kafka
        kafkaPublisher.publish("order-events", order.getOrderId(), "OrderCreated");
        return order; // Customer sees: "Order ORD-123 placed, processing..."
    }
}
```

### What is the outbox pattern and why is it needed?

The outbox pattern ensures the local state change and the event are atomic. Without it, you could commit the order but fail to publish the event, or publish but not commit.

```java
package com.bank.order.outbox;

import java.time.Instant;

public class OutboxEvent {
    private long id;
    private String aggregateId;    // orderId
    private String eventType;      // "OrderCreated"
    private String payload;        // JSON
    private boolean published;
    private Instant createdAt;

    // A relay process polls unpublished events and sends to Kafka
    // After successful publish, marks published = true
}
```

### How does idempotent order creation work?

The client sends an idempotency key with every create request. The order service stores processed keys, so a retry returns the existing order instead of creating a new one.

```java
package com.bank.order.service;

// WRONG: no idempotency check
public class OrderServiceWrong {
    public Order createOrder(String customerId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        // Every call creates a new order, even retries!
        return new Order(generateId(), customerId, amount, null);
    }
}

// CORRECT: idempotency key prevents duplicates
// (See OrderService.createOrder above with idempotencyKey check)
```

### How do you monitor the Saga?

Track each Saga instance's state and emit metrics for started, completed, compensated, and stuck Sagas. Alert on compensation rate and Sagas that have not progressed within expected time.

```java
package com.bank.order.monitoring;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class SagaMetrics {

    private final AtomicLong started = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong completed = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong compensated = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong stuck = new AtomicLong();

    public void onSagaStarted() { started.incrementAndGet(); }
    public void onSagaCompleted() { completed.incrementAndGet(); }
    public void onSagaCompensated() { compensated.incrementAndGet(); }
    public void onSagaStuck() { stuck.incrementAndGet(); }

    public void printMetrics() {
        System.out.printf("Sagas: started=%d, completed=%d, compensated=%d, stuck=%d%n",
            started.get(), completed.get(), compensated.get(), stuck.get());
    }
}
```

```text
Sagas: started=1250, completed=1200, compensated=45, stuck=5
```

### How do you scale the system?

Stateless services scale horizontally. Kafka partitions let consumers scale out, partitioned by order ID so all events for one order stay ordered. The money path stays strongly consistent while surrounding flows are eventual.

```text
# Kafka partitioning by order ID:
# Order ORD-001 → partition 0 → consumer-instance-A
# Order ORD-002 → partition 1 → consumer-instance-B
# All events for one order are ordered within its partition.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you handle a payment that succeeds but the confirmation event is lost? What happens if the compensation itself fails? How do you test a Saga end-to-end? What is the difference between orchestration and choreography? When would you choose choreography over orchestration?
